swer-field/id #uuid "67c84362-9ee8-5fb1-9cc5-4982acd43689" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413990} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7704adc1-0bd3-5a6a-8c14-5c138df4c7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7704adc1-0bd3-5a6a-8c14-5c138df4c7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413968} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca7af99b-f3db-551f-a410-e7335484fbb7" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413994} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6cea381-d89c-5a7b-a546-90c344bcd752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6cea381-d89c-5a7b-a546-90c344bcd752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413979} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8825eea-6236-5c45-a6bf-fa0d2e334802" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da6ab59f-cfd7-5d6f-94dd-f3fddb0a05c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-253391" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{3}(2))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $n=3,$ and $\\lambda =2.$\n\nSince $n=3,$ we need to compute the first $3-1=2$ derivative of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(2)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(2) \\\\ 0 & f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "706303c1-0e4c-55f7-8b62-5094938f85bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086b5f8c-361e-5e84-ba7f-07908a6ffad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-92 & 62 \\\\ -186 & 125\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413955} {:answer/id #uuid "5f32c9ba-7882-5683-b822-c627858b4d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}72 & -52 \\\\ -166 & 75\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413962} {:answer/id #uuid "6915da6f-7547-5e67-bf4a-3730ded795b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}62 & 92 \\\\ -125 & 186\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413964} {:answer/id #uuid "774e359d-a732-5eac-9259-4f31c4ad4889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}32 & 24 \\\\ -18 & 180\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413970} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0c78fd-212a-50ef-8305-029fa98e0cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}31 & 46 \\\\ -67 & 98\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "086b5f8c-361e-5e84-ba7f-07908a6ffad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-92 & 62 \\\\ -186 & 125\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413955} :answer-field/id #uuid "d52e4f2e-ac8e-5bd0-a3c8-e99ceefdc963" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a76ab5b-a059-5458-b5f4-e674816ed366" :question/math-academy-id "q-253488" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $X=PD{P}^{-1},$ find ${X}^{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)={x}^{5}$ and the given matrix $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n{X}^{5}=(PD{P}^{-1})^{5}=P\\cdot ({D}^{5})\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n{D}^{5}=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{5}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{2}{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{5} & =P\\cdot ({D}^{5})\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -96 & 64\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-92 & 62 \\\\ -186 & 125\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87883002-67eb-5111-b15a-c5941bb33f9b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf34227-df92-59d8-9613-ceb5484789cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bf34227-df92-59d8-9613-ceb5484789cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413956} :answer-field/id #uuid "25dde8d8-265c-54c9-ac3f-e40a69e95898" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c37bfdb-2dfe-5cc2-840b-243db54502a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c37bfdb-2dfe-5cc2-840b-243db54502a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413959} :answer-field/id #uuid "506c651f-f5b6-52a6-9177-275eb76f9e87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413988} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2be1c477-86a4-5471-a907-e291cdd9c2e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2be1c477-86a4-5471-a907-e291cdd9c2e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413957} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fabe0ba-2f06-5d33-ae09-be09b22d98ca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413992} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0ade4ad-0a6f-502d-8256-a7ee48405353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0ade4ad-0a6f-502d-8256-a7ee48405353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413981} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4498fa0-0077-5245-b464-34ae73a87b7d" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413996} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb01895-358d-5093-891c-8725486935f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cb01895-358d-5093-891c-8725486935f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413960} :answer-field/id #uuid "e436c140-e683-55ba-81fe-1b3bbe91bf3f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414001} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c827e8bb-8488-5496-9b36-15035f1d576c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c827e8bb-8488-5496-9b36-15035f1d576c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413980} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c61432-7357-5400-9143-63f15ef64c3f" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7819c8fe-793f-5300-ba05-a00d3d72cc5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-253884" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(0))}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(0))} & ={e}^{([\\begin{aligned}{J}_{1}(3) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{3}(0)\\end{aligned}])} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{1}(3))} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{({J}_{3}(0))}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{({J}_{1}(3))}\\,⊕\\,{e}^{({J}_{3}(0))}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(3),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =3.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(3)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(0),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(0)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(0) & {f}^{′}(0) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(0)}{2} \\\\ 0 & f(0) & {f}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(0)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{0} & {e}^{0} & \\frac{{e}^{0}}{2} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{0} & {e}^{0} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {e}^{0}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(0))} & ={e}^{({J}_{1}(3))}\\,⊕\\,{e}^{({J}_{3}(0))} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
